  <h1>Training Journal: Registered for Vegas, Six Weeks to Build</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">November 27, 2026 · Journal</div>

  <article>
    <p>Decision made: I am running the Vegas marathon on January 10.</p>

    <p>The entry was $199, and a 20 percent off code took about $40 off.
    That tipped it. A race I was only half sure about is now one I am
    committed to, and that changes how I train.</p>

    <h2>The catch</h2>
    <p>Early January is a tough date. The holidays are in the way, and my back
    is still tight. I used to run the Rock n Roll Arizona marathon on MLK
    weekend until they ended it, and that gave me an extra week compared to
    this date. One week sounds small, but in a short build it matters.</p>

    <p>So the plan needs a rethink. About six weeks is not a lot of time, but it
    is enough to do something useful: get a long run in, keep the back
    settled, and avoid getting hurt. The goal is to start the race healthy
    and let the pace follow.</p>

    <h2>What I am working out next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>How long my long runs can get in six weeks without wrecking the back</li>
      <li>What the holidays leave me for training days</li>
      <li>Whether the 4:02 the calculator gave me from the 10 miler is still the
      best case, or whether it should be 4:05 or slower</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I will report back with the plan, and how the first long run goes.</p>

    <p>For reference, here is the
    <a href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=even&amp;t=4:05:00&amp;eq=half&amp;eqt=1:49:56&amp;profile=1.090">4:05 plan</a>,
    about 9:20 per mile.</p>
